==== How to Play

This chapter is the complete beginner tutorial for Wave 0. It assumes you have never played No Man's Sky and have *no mods active yet* — just the vanilla game at version 6.45.1. Wave 0 exists to teach the game; the mods you add to it only make the game easier to see, operate, and plan.

===== The Story So Far

*First Light.* The star you were chasing has burned out — or so you thought. Where its light should have ended, you found a glimmer instead: a thread of pale fire stretched between dead worlds, humming with a frequency your suit has started to translate into names you do not remember choosing. Your ship is down. Your memories begin at the impact. Somewhere ahead, a light is waiting to be found, and every star chart you will ever draw starts from this one waking moment. This is your first day in the universe, and the universe is enormous.

===== Your First Moments

You wake beside a wrecked starship on an alien world. The game teaches as you go — this section tells you what the prompts mean.

+ *Move and look* — standard first-person controls; your keybinds are shown on screen and rebindable in *Options*. Press the jetpack key (Space by default) to boost, and hold it to glide. Watch your fuel — you can see the jetpack meter while airborne.
+ *Scan* — the scan key (C by default) pulses your surroundings and reveals nearby resources and points of interest. Scan often; scanning is how you *see*.
+ *Analysis visor* — the visor key (F by default) zooms into a detailed view: it names the flora and fauna around you, highlights resource deposits, and marks where to go next.
+ *Mining beam* — hold the fire button on rocks, plants, and crystals to harvest them. Resources stream into your inventory automatically. Rocks give ferrite, plants give carbon, glowing plants and hazards give oxygen.
+ *Interact* — the interact key (E by default) opens doors, picks things up, talks to terminals, and enters your ship.

#callout("Tip:", [
  The two meters that matter at the very start are *life support* and *hazard protection*, both bottom-right on your HUD. Life support drains slowly no matter what and is refilled with *oxygen*. Hazard protection drains on hostile worlds and is refilled with *sodium* (the yellow glowing plants). Refill either from the quick menu (X by default). If either runs dry, your *health* starts draining instead — that is the panic state you want to avoid.
])

===== The Survival Loop

Every early session is the same loop, in different clothes: *gather → refuel → survive → repair → fly.*

+ *Carbon* — from plants. Fuel for your mining beam's overheat recovery, refining, and early crafting. Never leave base without a stack.
+ *Ferrite dust* — from rocks. The backbone of repairs and base building.
+ *Oxygen* — from plants, oxygen-rich hazards, and some crystals. Life support fuel and a refining ingredient.
+ *Sodium* — from yellow plants on most worlds. Hazard protection fuel; on extreme worlds, carry a lot.

Your two meters are the clock: when they dip, top them up *before* they empty. Dying is recoverable (you respawn at your ship or last save point), but it costs you your current loot run, so treat the meters as the tutorial's way of teaching you to prepare.

#callout("Tip:", [
  Save often: exiting your ship saves, and so do save points and beacons. Early on, the habit of "get out of the ship = save" will save you more than any mod ever will.
])

===== First Flight

The early missions walk you through repairing your ship. The two repairs that matter first:

+ *Launch thrusters* — takeoff fuel. Craft *Launch Fuel* (40 *Di-hydrogen* + 1 *Metal Plating*) and refuel from the ship's inventory screen. Di-hydrogen comes from the blue crystals on most planets; metal plating is crafted from ferrite.
+ *Pulse engine* — the in-system drive that makes travel between planets fast. Hold the boost key once airborne and you streak across the sky; release when you reach the planet you want.

Takeoff is automatic once your thrusters are fuelled — the ship lifts when you boost from the ground. Landing is manual or auto: aim at a landing pad, or let the game set you down on clear ground.

#callout("Tip:", [
  The moment you can fly, the *quick menu* (X by default) becomes your best friend: it holds summoning (your ship, later exocraft), refuelling shortcuts, and the camera. Learn it early.
])

===== The Core Quest Lines

Your *mission log* (Esc → Mission Log) tracks everything. There are three story threads to know at the beginner level:

+ *Awakenings* — the tutorial line. It takes you from the wrecked ship to your first space station and unlocks the fundamentals. Follow it until it ends; it is your onboarding.
+ *The Atlas Path* — the philosophical main line about the universe's makers. It hands out blueprints and lore; you can pursue it whenever you want.
+ *The Artemis Path* — the long-form story that begins after you have made a few hyperdrive jumps. This is the "real" campaign for most players.

#callout("Note:", [
  The *Anomaly* — the circular hub you can summon from the quick menu — is the multiplayer and endgame hub: the Nexus mission board, quicksilver shop, and the base-building specialists live there. You unlock the ability to summon it early in the story; once it is available, use it.
])

===== Base Building Basics

Your first base is your home port, and it can be small.

+ *Base computer* — claim a spot by building the computer and using it. From then on the area is yours.
+ *Power* — *solar panels* plus *batteries* are the reliable pair (day/night cycle); the *biofuel reactor* is the early stopgap. Nothing important runs without power, so build power first.
+ *Teleporter* — build one at your base; it links to the space stations you visit. This is your fast-travel network.
+ *Refiner* — turns raw resources into better ones (carbon → condensed carbon, ferrite → pure ferrite, and so on). It is the gateway to advanced crafting.
+ *Save point* — one small structure that saves on demand. Build one beside your door.

#callout("Tip:", [
  Your first base does not need to be pretty — it needs to be *useful*: power, a refiner, a teleporter, a save point. You can build your dream base later, somewhere beautiful.
])

===== Crafting & Inventory

+ *Units* — the everyday currency. Earned by selling surplus resources and discoveries; spent on ships, upgrades, and supplies.
+ *Nanites* — the upgrade currency. Earned from scanning discoveries, missions, and some interactions; spent on technology and upgrade modules.
+ *Quicksilver* — the special currency of the Anomaly. Earned from Nexus missions and expeditions; spent on cosmetic and exotic items.

Your inventory has four homes: *exosuit, ship, multi-tool, freighter* (the freighter comes later). Expand the exosuit early — one extra slot per space station (costs units), and free slots from *drop pods* found by scanning planets.

#callout("Tip:", [
  Refining is where the real value is. Carbon → *condensed carbon* doubles its usefulness; ferrite → *pure ferrite* unlocks better building. Get comfortable with the refiner in Wave 0 and the later waves' economy will feel natural.
])

===== New-Game Checklist

1. Confirm the main menu reads version 6.45.1.
2. Confirm Singularity has exactly Wave 0's mods enabled — and nothing else.
3. Start a *new* game on the difficulty you want (Normal is the intended Wave 0 experience).
4. Follow *Awakenings* to the space station.
5. Claim your first base; build power, refiner, teleporter, save point.
6. Save by getting out of your ship, often.
7. When the story prompts you to jump star systems, that is Wave 1's door — close it for now and enjoy learning to fly.
